[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi! I'm working on a project which is based on mathlib's computability theory library. I'm looking at the <code>eval</code> function in <code>PartrecCode.lean</code> and have a question.</p>\n<p>Here is what it does for <code>rfind'</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Code</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→.</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfind'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">unpaired</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">rfind</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;$&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">pair</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Here is what it says it does:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c1\">-- * `Nat.Partrec.Code.rfind'`: Minimization. For `f` an argument of the form</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- `Nat.pair a m`, `rfind' f m` returns the least `a` such that `f a m = 0`, if one</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- exists and `f b m` terminates for `b &lt; a`</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I don't think this makes sense. Here is what I think it does:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c1\">-- * `Nat.Partrec.Code.rfind'`: Minimization starting at a provided value.</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Given an argument of the form `Nat.pair a m`, returns the least `n ≥ m`</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- such that `eval cf (pair a n) = 0`, if such an `n` exists and if `eval cf (pair a k)`</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- terminates for `m ≤ k ≤ n`.</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Can someone confirm? I'm happy to submit a PR if so.</p>",
        "id": 580045270,
        "sender_full_name": "Jacob Parish",
        "timestamp": 1773780509
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110049\">@Mario Carneiro</span> would have the definitive answer here... imho the existing doc string could use some improvement in general</p>",
        "id": 580049292,
        "sender_full_name": "Matt Diamond",
        "timestamp": 1773782055
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm inclined to agree with your interpretation</p>",
        "id": 580049929,
        "sender_full_name": "Matt Diamond",
        "timestamp": 1773782285
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think the existing description would apply to <code>rfind</code> but this is about <code>rfind'</code></p>",
        "id": 580051120,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1773782702
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I thought that too, but even if it was about <code>rfind</code> I think it's not quite right? For instance, the argument to <code>rfind</code> isn't paired.</p>",
        "id": 580054251,
        "sender_full_name": "Jacob Parish",
        "timestamp": 1773783977
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hmm you're right this doesn't make too much sense; perhaps this comment used to be correct but the definition got changed?</p>",
        "id": 580054566,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1773784112
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This code hasn't changed <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib3/commit/f0bcba56f90e5a26fcb537988224a43170942ae8\">since this file was created</a>.</p>\n<p>The docstring also hasn't changed <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib3/commit/132ea0510de53cc05a05d582476cfff8d06d27ff\">since it was added</a>.</p>\n<p>So <code>rfind' f m</code> seems to be a typo.</p>",
        "id": 580068024,
        "sender_full_name": "Miyahara Kō",
        "timestamp": 1773791010
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"766618\">@Jacob Parish</span> <br>\nI believe your suggestion is correct.<br>\nI'd be happy to review if you created a PR.</p>\n<p>FYI: I will make <code>Partrec</code> a <code>fun_prop</code> decl in the near future, to finish the proof of the Matiyasevich theorem.</p>",
        "id": 580069240,
        "sender_full_name": "Miyahara Kō",
        "timestamp": 1773791814
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Just made one here: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/36780\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/36780</a></p>",
        "id": 580070323,
